WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.120
This is Robert Sheer with another edition of Sheer Intelligence, it's actually my alternative

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:33.360
to the Central Intelligence Agency and by coincidence our guest today is John Kiriako who was sort of

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.840
a hero at the Central Intelligence Agency where he worked for almost 15 years and he

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:48.160
overlapped the events of 9-11 and it was John Kiriako who was in charge of counter-terrorism

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:56.480
operations in Pakistan and as an Arabic speaker and very knowledgeable in the region who was

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:02.720
involved in the capture of the most important, at that time most important Al Qaeda operative

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:12.560
supposed to have been the number 3-1 Abu Zubedah and the interesting question here is the effectiveness

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:21.120
of torture and the nominee from President Trump to be the new head of the CIA, Gina Haspel has

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:28.240
been referred to some including John Kiriako as bloody Gina because during that period she was a

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:34.400
deputy director of counter-terrorism activities and she was involved in not only conducting

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:42.400
these torture experiments and practices in Thailand and elsewhere but she is also accused

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.960
of having destroyed 92 tapes that were supposed to be released about the torture program which

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.520
don't exist now so why don't you bring us up to date we're doing this interview while she's

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.480
still the nominee and what is your view of that nomination and where do you think it's going

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:02.480
to end up? First of all thanks for having me Bob it's always a pleasure to speak with you I

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:07.680
enjoy these conversations a great deal and I think this is a very very important issue

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:13.200
I've been writing about this and speaking about this extensively I did an op-ed in the

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:20.800
Washington Post last week I feel very very strongly about this nominee and my feeling is

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.320
that it's a wonderful wonderful thing that the president wants to name a woman as the director

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:33.200
of the CIA but that woman ought not to be Gina Haspel there are probably 50 women across government

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:39.600
across the intelligence community who would be well qualified highly qualified and would

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:46.880
probably be terrific directors of the CIA but Gina Haspel should be disqualified for her past

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.920
at the very top of the CIA's illegal torture program well why don't you bring us up to date

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:57.600
on that because you know yes some people are even saying you should support her because she is a

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:04.080
woman and you know there are other people saying she just was following orders and what

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:11.600
have you and you've written quite critically of that practice of exonerating torture so what's

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.680
going on here are we have we come to see torture as American as apple pie I know a group of defense

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:23.520
intellectuals who opposed torture have cited polls saying that a majority of Americans now

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.520
think it's an efficient tool to make us more secure we seem to be the major force in the

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:36.400
world justifying torture these days so what's happened here yeah that's that's a shame too because

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:43.600
the torture issue has become very partisan a huge majority of republicans supports the

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:49.600
torture program and a huge majority of democrats opposes the torture program so let's get a couple

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:58.080
of our of our historical facts out on the table at first in 1946 bob we executed Japanese

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.960
soldiers who had waterboarded American prisoners of war during the second world war

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:07.520
it wasn't called waterboarding then it was called water torture or the water treatment

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:13.040
but it was waterboarding plain and simple and that was deemed to be a death penalty offense

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:20.720
in january of 1968 the washington post ran a front page photograph of an american soldier

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.080
waterboarding a north vietnamese prisoner of war on the day that that photo was published

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:32.160
the secretary of defense robert macnamara ordered an investigation the soldier was arrested he was

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:38.880
charged with torture he was convicted and he was sentenced to 20 years in leavenworth well

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:46.640
between 1946 and now the law never changed waterboarding and other forms of torture

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:56.240
were illegal in 1946 they were illegal in 1968 and john uh you and j byby's memo uh from the justice

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:04.000
department in 2002 notwithstanding waterboarding was still illegal in 2002 so you know to me

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:10.880
we are conceding our leadership position on international human rights and we have decided

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:16.320
as a matter of policy that whatever is expedient is okay because we're the good guys

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:22.400
and so we can do whatever we want and that's just simply wrong i mean clearly we're hardly the good

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:29.040
guys in wars like iraq that you that's right i know about which we're done without any justification

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:35.360
fake data which by the way was supposed to have been collected in part by torture and it was

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:41.440
false data but but the argument that and it's interesting and and the defensive torture the

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:47.280
public has changed on this and partially that's due of course of of hollywood and mass media and it

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:54.400
will be like zero dark 30 which we're where the cia took hollywood director catlin bill glow and her

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:59.280
screenwriters and others into their confidence actually betrayed national security by giving

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.240
them the names of navy seals who were later involved in the capture bin laden and somehow

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:09.360
got hollywood to endorse or that part of hollywood to endorse the idea of torture

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.800
as efficient but you know from your own experience that it's been exactly the opposite it created

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:21.120
bad information so why don't you tell us about the example of your own capture of abu zubeda and

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:28.000
and and what you learned about the whole process sure we captured abu zubeda in uh march late

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:35.440
march of 2002 he had been shot and severely wounded shot by a pakistani policeman with an ak-47

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:42.240
in the thigh the groin in the stomach he was very gravely injured and we held him in pakistan for about

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:48.720
four days three and a half days after he was captured and i i spent 54 consecutive hours

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:54.080
with him just the two of us in his hospital room i had tied him to his bed because i was

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:58.720
afraid that i would fall asleep and he would somehow escape when he finally came out of

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:06.000
this coma after he was shot we we began conversing and we talked about a lot of things we talked about

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:12.800
the september 11 attacks we talked about uh his family uh he had been writing poetry he wanted

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:17.840
to recite poetry we talked about um christianity and islam we talked about a lot of different

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:25.840
things well i told him that uh and i said it very plainly i said listen i'm the nicest guy

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:32.000
that you're going to encounter in this in this experience my colleagues are not as nice as i am

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:38.160
so if i can give you one piece of advice it's this it's that you have to cooperate because

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:46.560
your life is over so the rest of it can be terrible or it can be okay it's up to you

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:52.640
i urge you to cooperate and he said to me you seem like a nice man but you're the enemy

00:07:52.640 --> 00:08:00.960
and i'll never cooperate well i said suit yourself in the meantime a cia jet came in in the middle of

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:06.880
the night three fbi agents and i picked him up uh on a gurney and we carried him out to the plane

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:11.360
we took him to the back of the plane and strapped him down to the luggage rack and i said to him

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:17.600
remember you have to cooperate and he squeezed my hand i never saw him again but what happened

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:24.480
subsequently is really what lays the foundation of this entire debate and that is when he arrived at

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:31.600
his uh secret prison site that's been discussed extensively in the press he was too severely

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:38.240
wounded to be interrogated so the cia gave him about six weeks to recover from his wounds

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:43.600
at the end of that six week period an fbi agent by the name of ali sufan began to

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:51.760
interrogate him and ali sufan used a tried and true fbi technique and that technique is just simply

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:58.320
to establish a rapport with your subject to to establish a relationship to the point where

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:03.600
your prisoner feels comfortable enough that he inevitably will open up to you and will begin

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:10.720
talking well that's exactly what happened ali sufan engaged abu zubeda in conversation

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:16.800
he offered him some dates he offered him a cigarette or an orange or whatever it was

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:23.520
and over the period of several weeks abu zubeda began to open up and he gave ali sufan what ali

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:29.600
later described as actionable intelligence that was used to disrupt future attacks and to save

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:36.080
american lives well the cia for whatever reason decided that this was taking too long the cia

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:41.040
had convinced itself that there was another attack that was imminent and the only way to get the

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:49.360
information on that attack was to torture abu zubeda and so in july of 2002 george tenet who

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:56.000
was the director of the cia at the time went to president bush and asked president bush to move

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:06.960
authority over abu zubeda from the fbi

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.680
to the CIA. Now usually overseas the CIA has primacy and domestically the FBI has

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:11.080
primacy but because the September 11th attacks were still considered to be an

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:16.240
open criminal investigation the FBI had primacy in this case. Now for whatever

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:20.520
reason and he's never explained this President Bush decided to do just that

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:26.240
and to transfer primacy from the FBI to the CIA that happened on August the

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:32.600
1st 2002 within two hours of that transfer of primacy the CIA began to

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:37.720
torture Abu Zubaydah and he immediately clammed up. Now when I say torture there

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:41.280
were 10 separate techniques that were supposed to be used on Abu Zubaydah and

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:46.760
the CIA officers implementing this program were supposed to start with the

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:51.840
mildest technique and that was grabbing him by the lapels giving him

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:57.000
a shake and yelling at him to answer their questions but they didn't do that they

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.560
start they started with some of the roughest techniques the first one was

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:05.800
called walling where a prisoner is supposed to have a towel wrapped around

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:11.760
his neck and he slammed into into a wall a wooden wall in order to

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:15.480
frighten him it makes a loud sound your head slams against the wall it's

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:19.400
it's shocking the problem is is that Abu Zubaydah was never given a towel

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:24.080
around his neck and he was slammed into a concrete wall we know from other

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:28.160
prisoners who were subjected to walling that they have suffered permanent brain

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:33.320
damage from this technique later that very same day Abu Zubaydah was water

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:39.680
boarded for the first time for the first of 83 times so he became so

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:44.840
enraged that he just stopped talking all together and the more the CIA

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:50.720
tortured him the angrier he became but he refused to cooperate finally the

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:55.000
torturers who who were two contract psychologists named Mitchell and

00:11:55.000 --> 00:12:01.160
Jessen decided that they needed to bring Ali Sufran back into the country so

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:06.920
Ali Sufran and an FBI contingent flew back to this secret location it took

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:11.680
Ali another two or three weeks to reestablish that rapport and again

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:16.680
Abu Zubaydah began to talk now we know from the Senate torture report that the

