00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:32.980
my attorneys where they declined to prosecute me. But what I did not know was that a month

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:39.940
later in January of 2009, when President Obama was inaugurated, that the CIA asked

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:47.260
him to secretly reopen the case against me. And he did. And so I had no idea that for the

00:21:47.260 --> 00:21:52.620
next three years, my phones were tapped, my emails were being intercepted. I had three

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:59.460
different teams of FBI agents surveilling me and following me. And then finally in

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:06.380
January of 2012, I was charged with these five felonies and facing 45 years in prison,

00:22:06.380 --> 00:22:11.340
which is essentially a death penalty. I agreed to take a plea to this lesser charge to make

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:12.340
this thing go away.

00:22:12.340 --> 00:22:19.620
You're the one person that suffers time in jail for having revealed the existence of

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:24.980
a torture program. And now Gina Haspel is on the verge of being rewarded with being

00:22:24.980 --> 00:22:32.100
head of the director of the leading intelligence agency in the world, the most powerful.

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:38.580
And we're being asked as a public to ignore her involvement in this torture program. And

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:44.140
I was wondering, you worked in the Senate with John Kerry, his committee, he hired you,

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:49.620
you know, your master's thesis was on the legislative process, and you work with a whole

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:55.140
bunch of defense intellectuals and former intelligence officials who have condemned

00:22:55.140 --> 00:23:01.660
this torture. And Senator John McCain, himself, was held as a prisoner in North Vietnam,

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:07.420
condemned this behavior. What response are you getting? Are they telling you that we should

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:12.820
look the other way because Gina Haspel is sorry for what she did or she's a woman or it doesn't

00:23:12.820 --> 00:23:14.660
matter? What are you hearing?

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:19.120
I'm sad to tell you that, yes, for the most part, most of the people that I'm engaging

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:22.700
with are saying we need to move on from this. And I'll tell you where I've been

00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:28.020
the most disappointed too. Number one, Chuck Schumer, the Senate minority leader, announced

00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:33.220
last week that he would not whip votes for this confirmation hearing. And what that means

00:23:33.220 --> 00:23:39.100
is that he is not instructing Democratic senators to vote against Gina Haspel. He's telling them

00:23:39.100 --> 00:23:44.260
that they can vote their conscience. Well, when you have five Democrats running for reelection

00:23:44.260 --> 00:23:49.380
in states that Donald Trump won by at least 10 percentage points, that means go ahead

00:23:49.380 --> 00:23:55.220
and vote yes on Gina Haspel. So right off the top, the vote is probably lost.

00:23:55.220 --> 00:24:01.340
The only hope I think that we have are people like Rand Paul, who not only announced that

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:06.500
he would vote no, but also announced that he would filibuster Gina Haspel's nomination.

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:07.500
That's a no.

00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:13.820
Rand Paul, the Republican and Libertarian Senator from Kentucky, who was opposed to

00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:19.500
the war in Iraq and from the beginning anyway, and many of these foreign adventures.

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:24.460
But let me ask you about your grasp on reality here. Is any of something incredibly

00:24:24.460 --> 00:24:30.580
shocking? You are somebody who were punished, as I said, for revealing torture. We've signed

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:37.100
these international agreements, even under a sort of hawkish president like Ronald Reagan,

00:24:37.100 --> 00:24:43.260
against torture. And yet we're about to anoint this woman to be head of our most secret

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:50.340
program, who is what you call a bloody Gina, but she certainly has bloody hands in

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:57.180
this respect. And in terms of, again, these senators and so forth, I mean, they might want

00:24:57.180 --> 00:25:02.420
to get reelected, but what's going on here? How did torture, you signed some of your articles

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:07.740
that there's a popular support for torture. How did this come to be?

00:25:07.740 --> 00:25:15.500
I remember in 2007 seeing a poll that said that a majority of Americans opposed torturing

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:22.820
prisoners, but a majority of Americans supported killing them. And I remember being appalled

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:27.740
by that. We were supposed to be a nation of laws. We're supposed to be a nation of law

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:33.780
and order and respect for the Constitution. And when you capture people and you torture

00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:39.500
them and you hold them in communicado for decades at a secret prison where even the

00:25:39.500 --> 00:25:44.340
Red Cross doesn't have access, that's not respect for the rule of law. And you

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:49.380
know, how do we go to our allies and friends around the world with a straight face every

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:55.180
year when we write the State Department's a congressionally mandated human rights report

00:25:55.180 --> 00:26:00.100
and we tell them, you have to respect international human rights. And then they say to us, but

00:26:00.100 --> 00:26:06.220
you don't respect human rights. So, you know, I talked to a foreign diplomat just recently

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:12.540
and he said that he had an almost surreal experience where he had a State Department

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:19.500
officer come into his office and essentially scold him and say, you have to respect international

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:24.900
human rights. And when that State Department officer left his office, a CIA officer walked

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:29.380
into the office and said, listen, we want to send a prisoner over to your place. We

00:26:29.380 --> 00:26:33.220
want you to, you know, manhandle him and let us know what he says.

00:26:33.220 --> 00:26:37.900
And the person who ran those programs of rendition, sending those prisoners to

00:26:37.900 --> 00:26:44.780
be tortured in Egypt and Thailand and all sorts of places, that is the woman that was

00:26:44.780 --> 00:26:52.380
nominated by our president to be the new director of the CIA, Gina Haspel. And you make

00:26:52.380 --> 00:26:57.380
a pretty serious charge. You actually say this woman was not some bystander or a lower

00:26:57.380 --> 00:27:03.860
level official, but she was the number two person running the torture program.

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:10.260
That's right. She was. She was the number two. And again, the message to the CIA workforce is

00:27:10.260 --> 00:27:14.180
that you don't have to respect the law. The message is that you don't have to

00:27:14.180 --> 00:27:18.980
respect the oversight committees because the oversight committees are largely cheerleaders

00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:25.380
or lemmings and they'll do as they're told to do. And we see this proven time and time again.

00:27:26.180 --> 00:27:30.500
One of my attorneys spoke recently with Senator Ron Wyden of Oregon

00:27:30.500 --> 00:27:35.860
to ask him if he was going to join in the filibuster with Rand Paul against the Gina

00:27:35.860 --> 00:27:42.740
Haspel nomination. And Wyden responded, look, I'd like to, but it takes all my energy

00:27:43.380 --> 00:27:49.060
just to not lose my security clearance. And so to me, that's not leadership.

00:27:49.060 --> 00:27:53.860
That's not oversight. That's allowing the CIA to set the agenda. And then

00:27:53.860 --> 00:27:59.300
the overseers are commanded to fall into line. And time and time again, they do.

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:03.140
We should be clear about Ron Wyden is a guy that, you know, I've certainly long respected.

00:28:03.140 --> 00:28:07.940
He's been one of the few individuals on the Senate Intelligence Committee who's

00:28:07.940 --> 00:28:13.620
actually brought up the issue of torture. And you have people, Diane Feinstein is given credit

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:16.900
because she was the chair of the Committee, although for five years she knew about

00:28:16.900 --> 00:28:22.500
the torture. Didn't tell us about it. But nonetheless, they printed a heavily redacted

00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:25.860
form of it. We've never actually seen the report. You have people, again,

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:30.900
on the House side Intelligence Committee like Adam Schiff, who are very concerned about foreign

00:28:30.900 --> 00:28:37.300
governments, you know, killing people in their secret agencies where they form agent in England

00:28:37.300 --> 00:28:42.180
and the part of Putin's Russia and so forth. And yet you're basically saying these intelligence

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:48.340
agencies that are supposed to be monitoring the CIA and holding it accountable are in fact puppets

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:53.380
of it. Oh, they are puppets. You know, I remember at the height of the torture program,

00:28:53.380 --> 00:28:59.620
I was working still working in the CIA's counterterrorism center. And I remember Jane Harmon

00:28:59.620 --> 00:29:05.220
coming to the building for briefings. And these were very high level, very, you know,

00:29:05.220 --> 00:29:10.740
several compartments above top secret briefings on this program. And then when it was finally

00:29:10.740 --> 00:29:17.380
exposed in 2007, she denied any knowledge of it. Well, I knew that that was a bald faced lie,

00:29:17.380 --> 00:29:21.940
just like when Nancy Pelosi denied that she had any knowledge of it. That was also a lie.

00:29:21.940 --> 00:29:28.180
Why was it alive? Nancy Pelosi, who we maybe think of as a great liberal, right? Well, Nancy Pelosi

00:29:28.180 --> 00:29:33.940
at the time was was the the House, what was it minority leader, I suppose, or majority leader,

00:29:33.940 --> 00:29:39.940
one or the other. And she had been personally briefed on the CIA's torture program personally

00:29:39.940 --> 00:29:45.540
briefed. The people who were briefed were the Speaker of the House, the majority leader,

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:50.900
the minority leader, the minority whip, and the chairman and vice chairman of the two committees.

00:29:51.780 --> 00:29:57.940
So she was personally briefed. Now her her defense, when this was finally made public,

00:29:57.940 --> 00:29:59.940
was she may have been invited

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:07.360
to the briefing but she probably left before the really bloody information was conveyed

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:12.480
and the assistant who had stayed behind to take notes apparently didn't brief her.

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:17.360
Well that's just nonsense because staff members were not privy to this information.

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:22.160
There was no staff member there taking notes. She was briefed, she walked out,

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:26.320
she didn't do anything to stop the torture program and then she denied any knowledge of it.

00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:31.600
Is this true of Dianne Feinstein as well? Another absolutely yes. Democratic,

00:30:31.600 --> 00:30:36.800
somewhat liberal leader? Well-known cheerleader for the CIA, Dianne Feinstein. I'll tell you another

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:42.640
thing about Dianne Feinstein. When Barack Obama was flailing around looking for a CIA director,

00:30:42.640 --> 00:30:46.240
you remember there were three or four people who were who were proposed and who

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:50.960
ended up never being able to make it through confirmation. Dianne Feinstein said that

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:57.920
she would vote yes for whomever President Obama wanted to name so long as Steve Kappus was named

00:30:57.920 --> 00:31:03.920
deputy director. So Steve Kappus had been the acting director. He had been the deputy director for

00:31:03.920 --> 00:31:10.880
operations, highly, highly respected, very professional officer, but he was also instrumental

00:31:10.880 --> 00:31:15.120
in the torture program. He was the deputy director for operations when the torture

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:19.760
program was being carried out. So why in the world would Dianne Feinstein,

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:26.720
if she was opposed to torture, demand, insist that one of the highest ranking officers involved

00:31:26.720 --> 00:31:32.400
in the torture program be named the CIA's deputy director? It just doesn't make any sense at all.

00:31:32.960 --> 00:31:38.800
So what is finally your opinion of what we learned from the torture report and how does it relate

00:31:38.800 --> 00:31:44.640
to Gina Haspel? Tell us about the torture report because what we have is the executive summary,

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:50.960
again, heavily redacted. That's right. We've got a heavily redacted 500-page executive summary

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:58.320
of a report that is supposed to be 5,000 to 7,000 pages long. Now, I've said this before.

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:03.200
I'm going to say it again because it's very important. If you look at the executive summary

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:08.240
that's been released and published, and it's available free now online thanks to the Haspel

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:15.440
nomination, pay very close and special attention to the footnotes because that's where the real story

00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:21.520
is. If you read the footnotes, you'll be able to figure out the entire story. And the story is

00:32:21.520 --> 00:32:27.760
that Gina Haspel was involved in this program up to her neck. And one of the objections that I

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:35.120
have to this nomination is that she had multiple opportunities over the course of several years

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:43.360
to say no, to say this is wrong, it's illegal, it's unconstitutional, it's unethical and immoral.

00:32:43.360 --> 00:32:52.160
And she never said any of that. She said, yes, sir, when told to destroy the tapes. Yes, sir,

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:56.080
when told to fly to the secret site to be chief of the torture program there.

00:32:56.640 --> 00:32:58.880
She could have said no, and she didn't.

00:32:59.680 --> 00:33:06.480
I want to thank you, John Kuriyaku, a 15-year veteran of the CIA who was rewarded for his

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:10.960
patriotic service and telling the American people the truth by putting an extent to

00:33:10.960 --> 00:33:17.120
federal prison and commenting on a woman who ran the program. And it may now be rewarded with

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:22.400
being head of the CIA. So thanks for coming. Thanks for having me. It's always a pleasure

00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:28.320
speaking with you. Our producers for the show, KCRW, are Rebecca Mooney and Josh Shear,

00:33:28.320 --> 00:33:35.920
our engineers are Kat Yor and Mario Diaz. See you next week for another edition of Shear Intelligence.

